<div style="margin-top:16px;max-width:900px;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e8e5e0;border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px;font:13px/1.6 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#3f3d39">
<div style="font:600 11px/1 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Helvetica,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.1em;color:#8a8680;margin-bottom:10px">THE RULES THAT MAKE IT SURVIVE 200</div>
<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:12px 26px">
<div><b style="color:#1c1b19">Waiting on you is never capped and never collapsed.</b> It is the reason the page exists. If it ever grows past a screenful, that is a real problem and hiding it would be the wrong answer.</div>
<div><b style="color:#1c1b19">Running and stopped cap at 3, oldest first, with an honest remainder link.</b> "2 more stopped" is a count you can act on; an infinite scroll of green rows is not.</div>
<div><b style="color:#1c1b19">Finished collapses to a count.</b> 183 delivered stories are an archive, not a queue. It stays one click away and appears in filter results.</div>
<div><b style="color:#1c1b19">The filter is the escape hatch, not a nav tree.</b> No epic grouping, no folders, no tabs — one field over key and title, with per-group "n of m match" so you always know what the filter is hiding.</div>
<div><b style="color:#1c1b19">Sort within group is by the thing that matters:</b> waiting longest first, most recent output first, quietest longest first. Never alphabetical, never by directory order.</div>
<div><b style="color:#1c1b19">Order is stable across the 3s poll.</b> A story only moves when its group changes — otherwise a running list re-sorts under the cursor every refresh.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<sc-if value="{{ showNotes }}" hint-placeholder-val="{{ true }}">
<div style="flex:none;width:320px;position:sticky;top:16px;font:13px/1.55 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#3f3d39">
<div style="border-left:2px solid #cfcbc4;padding:2px 0 2px 16px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
<div><div style="font:600 10px/1 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;letter-spacing:.1em;color:#8a8680;margin-bottom:6px">WHAT YOUR DOUBT WAS RIGHT ABOUT</div><p style="margin:0;text-wrap:pretty">Grouping by state was sound; <b>giving every group the same right to space was not</b>. At 6 stories the sidebar is a list. At 200 it is three different things stacked together — a queue, a monitor, and an archive — and only the first deserves the whole column.</p></div>
<div><p style="margin:0;text-wrap:pretty">Nothing here is a new state or a new inference. The groups, the words and the colours are exactly artboard <a href="#a1">01</a>'s. What is added is a cap, a count, and a filter.</p></div>
<div><p style="margin:0;text-wrap:pretty"><b>Finished is the one that actually grows without bound</b>, and it is the one thing on the page nobody needs to see. Collapsing it to a number reclaims most of the column at any scale.</p></div>
<div><p style="margin:0;text-wrap:pretty"><b>"2 more stopped" rather than a scroll.</b> A remainder link states its size, so a stalled backlog of 12 is visible as a fact even when only 3 are shown — the opposite of a list that quietly gets longer.</p></div>
<div><p style="margin:0;text-wrap:pretty">The per-group <b>"1 of 12 match"</b> line exists so a filter can never make you believe a group is empty. Filtered counts always name the total they came from.</p></div>
<div><p style="margin:0;text-wrap:pretty"><b>The table is the honest home for 210 stories.</b> The sidebar's job is the next decision; the full inventory belongs in the detail column where it can have columns and sorting.</p></div>
<div><span style="display:inline-block;padding:1px 6px;border-radius:4px;background:#fbf1dd;color:#8a5a00;font:600 10px/1.6 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;letter-spacing:.06em">OPEN QUESTION</span><p style="margin:6px 0 0;text-wrap:pretty">Does anything ever remove a directory from <span style="font:11.5px/1 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace">specs/</span>? If not, "finished" is permanent growth and the archive eventually wants a date grouping or a year filter. I would not build that until someone has 200 — but I would not pretend it will not happen either.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</sc-if>
</div>

<div id="a1" style="padding:24px 48px 12px;display:flex;gap:34px;align-items:flex-start;scroll-margin-top:16px">
